# Branch from up-to-date main

Before starting any new feature, fix, refactor, or other body of work, always bring `main` up to date and branch from it:

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b <branch-name>
```

**Why:** Branching from a stale local `main` causes PRs to be behind `origin/main`, which leads to unnecessary merge conflicts and out-of-date diffs.

**Rules:**
- Never branch from an existing feature branch unless the task is explicitly a follow-on to that branch's work.
- Always run `git pull origin main` — not just `git checkout main` — so the local ref is current before branching.
- If `main` has moved since a branch was cut and the branch is not yet pushed, rebase onto the updated `main` before opening the PR.
